{:event/id "b7195561-018f-4b60-bac0-966b07667d2e" :event/schema {:schema/root "8eb19ab0e4b70e205a84a627fd3e15c3ba79c0fd8c792d37e72f3c79511e0bf9" :schema/id :archaeology/finding-recorded :schema/hash "f2ad682fa2ac851a5d18b436849d3c59f07eb5f9ebe6179d231d7254b8d89848"} :event/type :archaeology/finding-recorded :event/stream "archaeology:findings:2302720f-6aae-405f-b0c9-520bcf34ae41" :event/seq 2 :event/causes ["16662ba4-d92c-4f84-b376-da390cb3103c"] :event/actor "agent:chatgpt" :event/subject "archaeology:2302720f-6aae-405f-b0c9-520bcf34ae41" :event/at "2026-09-05T13:25:22Z" :event/data {:finding/id :knoxx/review-write-has-compensation-bounded-core :finding/shapes #{:consistency :projection :state-machine :infra} :finding/current-name "record-segment-label! + review-one!" :finding/candidate-name "ReviewCommitBoundary<Fact,State,Projection>" :finding/claim "A review first persists a label fact and then mutates the segment status/current translation. If the segment write fails, Knoxx deletes the inserted label so the two core records do not remain observably divergent; the already-reserved label version may gap. Graph-memory refresh occurs after that core boundary and document review reports graph projection failure separately rather than rolling the review back. This is compensation-bounded core consistency plus a downstream projection, not an atomic transaction."}}
